+image_cache: reuse one connection per thread instead of one per image
+
+Every static asset was fetched with its own urllib.request.urlopen, so a
+badge whose median size is 5.6 KB paid a TCP connect and a TLS handshake of
+its own. Measured on a ROCKNIX guest against media.retroachievements.org:
+161 ms per image on a fresh connection against 28 ms over one already open,
+and 14.9 images/s through the real caching pass against 83.6/s with a
+connection per worker. For a first-time fill of a 494-game library -- 30,546
+images -- that is the difference between about half an hour of fetching and
+about six minutes, nearly all of it handshake.
+
+It is also the politer shape, which is why it is worth doing rather than
+raising concurrency: the same request count and the same bytes, a fraction
+of the connection setup. The pool stays at the size it was.
+
+A pooled connection can be closed by the server between uses and nothing
+says so until the next write fails, so every fetch retries once on a fresh
+connection. Response bodies are always read in full -- an unread body
+poisons the connection for the next caller -- and a non-2xx raises
+urllib.error.HTTPError exactly as urlopen did, so callers that treat any
+failure as "not cached" are unchanged. Redirects are followed up to three
+deep, cross-host included.
+
+The length and PNG checks added by 013 are untouched: the declared length
+now comes from the pooled response's headers, and nothing is published until
+it has passed both.
+
+Written for ROCKNIX (fork maxengel/rocknix#217).
